.\" Copyright (C) 2026 David Arnold
.TH DIDLCGEN 1 2026-10-30 DarqOS "DarqOS User's Manual"
.SH NAME
didlcgen \- generate C for DIPC messages from DIDL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B didlcgen
.RB [ \-d
.IR directory ]
.I input
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B didlcgen
reads the intermediate representation (IR) written by
.BR didlc (1)
and generates C for the messages it defines: a header and a source
file, named after the DIDL library with dots replaced by underscores,
so the library
.B darq.compositor
produces
.I darq_compositor.h
and
.IR darq_compositor.c .
The generated code uses the DIPC runtime,
.I darq/dipc.h
and
.I darq/dipc.c
(see
.BR FILES ).
It's C11, and needs a little-endian host.
.PP
Messages are used in place, in the buffer where they're built or
received: nothing is copied into, or out of, them.
So the header has a struct for each message, and for each DIDL struct,
laid out exactly as it is on the wire (see
.BR didl (5)),
with a
.B static_assert
checking each field's offset, and each struct's size, against the IR.
A message is built by assigning its fixed fields, and setting its
strings, opaques, sequences and ports with generated helpers, which
append their values; a received message is verified once, and then
read through a pointer to its struct.
.SS Names
Everything generated is prefixed with the library's name in lower
case,
.I L
below, and DIDL names are converted to snake case:
.B FileAttributes
becomes
.BR file_attributes .
.TP
.I enums
An integer typedef of the base type,
.IB L _ enum\fR,
a macro for each member,
.IB L _ ENUM _ MEMBER\fR,
in upper case, and
.IB L _ enum _valid( value )\fR,
true if a value is a member.
.TP
.I structs
.BI "struct " L _ struct\fR,
with its fields.
.TP
.I messages
For a method
.I x
with a response,
.BI "struct " L _ x _request
and
.BI "struct " L _ x _response\fR;
for a one-way method,
.BI "struct " L _ x\fR.
Each starts with a
.B struct darq_header
named
.BR header ,
holding the
.BR txid ,
the flags and the method number, followed by its fields.
.IB MESSAGE _METHOD
is its method number, and
.IB MESSAGE _LENGTH
the length of its fixed part, which may be a few bytes less than the
struct's size: the struct is aligned to at least 4 bytes, and its
trailing padding isn't part of the message.
.TP
.I fields
A number, bool or enum field is a member of its type:
.BR bool ,
.BR int8_t ,
\&...,
.BR uint64_t ,
.BR float ,
.BR double ,
or the enum's typedef.
A struct field is a member of the struct's type.
An
.BR opaque ,
.B string
or
.B sequence
is a
.BR "struct darq_ref" :
its value's offset from the start of the message, and its length (for
a string, without its NUL) or count of elements.
A
.B port
is a
.BR uint16_t :
an index into the message's handles.
.TP
.I protocols
.BI "const struct darq_method " L _ protocol _methods[]\fR,
listing each method's number, name, and its messages' lengths, with
.IB L _ PROTOCOL _NUM_METHODS
entries;
.BI "struct " L _ protocol _handler\fR,
of a function for each method, named
.BI handle_ method
in snake case; and
.IB L _ protocol _dispatch()\fR.
.SS Building
A message is built with a
.BR "struct darq_builder" ,
in a buffer that starts on an 8-byte boundary, and doesn't move while
it's built, such as a shared memory region: so pointers into it stay
valid.
.PP
.RS
.nf
_Alignas(8) uint8_t buffer[4096];
struct darq_builder builder;
darq_builder_init(&builder, buffer, sizeof(buffer));

struct example_files_list_request *m =
    example_files_list_request_init(&builder, txid);
m\->max = 100;
example_files_list_request_set_path(&builder, m, path, strlen(path));

/* Send builder.data, builder.length bytes, and builder.num_handles
 * handles from builder.handles. */
.fi
.RE
.PP
.IB MESSAGE _init( builder ", " txid )
writes the header and zeroes the fixed part, returning the message, or
NULL if the buffer's too small.
The fields are then set, in any order.
For each opaque, string, sequence and port field
.IR f ,
.IB MESSAGE _set_ f\fR(\fIbuilder\fR, \fImessage\fR, ...)
sets its value:
.TP
.B string
.RI ( "const char *value" ", " "size_t length" ):
it must be valid UTF-8, with no NUL.
.B darq_set_string_lossy()
sets text that may not be: invalid UTF-8, and NULs, are replaced with
U+FFFD, and it's cut short, at a character's boundary, at a length.
.TP
.B opaque
.RI ( "const void *value" ", " "size_t length" ).
.TP
.B sequence
.RI ( "uint32_t count" ):
returns the element array, zeroed, to fill in, or NULL.
An element that's a string, opaque or sequence is set with
.BR darq_set_string() ,
.B darq_set_opaque()
or
.BR darq_set_sequence() ,
given a pointer to it; a port, with
.BR darq_set_port() .
.TP
.B port
.RI ( "uint64_t handle" ):
the handle's index in
.BR builder.handles ,
added if it isn't there; handle 0 is no port.
.PP
A struct's fields have setters too:
.IB L _ struct _set_ f\fR(\fIbuilder\fR, \fIstruct\fR, ...),
for a struct within the message, or an element of a sequence.
The setters return 0, or
.B DARQ_DIPC_TOO_SMALL
if the buffer's full,
.B DARQ_DIPC_INVALID_VALUE
for a string that isn't valid, or
.BR DARQ_DIPC_TOO_MANY_HANDLES .
.SS Reading
A received message must be checked before it's used:
.IB MESSAGE _verify( data ", " length ", " num_handles )
returns 0, or what's wrong with it
.RB ( "enum darq_dipc_status" ;
.B darq_dipc_strerror()
describes it).
It checks everything
.BR didlpygen (1)'s
decoders check: that the message is 8-byte aligned and long enough,
with the right flags and method; that each value is 8-byte aligned,
within the message, and doesn't overlap another; that each string is
valid UTF-8, with no NUL, and followed by one; that each
.B bool
is 0 or 1, each enum value is a member, and each port index is within
the handles.
.PP
Then the message is read in place, through a pointer to its struct.
For each opaque, string, sequence and port field
.IR f ,
.IB MESSAGE _ f ( message )
returns its value: a
.B "const char *"
for a string, which is NUL-terminated, a pointer to the bytes of an
opaque, or to the elements of a sequence, with
.IB message -> f .length
their length or count; a port's takes the handles too, and returns the
handle, or 0.
A struct's take the message, as
.IB L _ struct _ f ( message ", " struct )\fR,
since values' offsets are from the message's start.
.B darq_string()
and the like read a sequence's elements.
.PP
Verifying a message protects its reader only while its sender can't
change it.
.SS Dispatching
.IB L _ protocol _dispatch( handler ", " context ", " data ", " length ", "
.IB handles ", " num_handles ", " reply )
verifies a request, and calls the handler's function for it, with the
context, the request, in place, and its handles.
A function for a method with a response builds it in
.IR reply ,
with the request's
.BR txid ,
and returns 0, or an error code
.RB ( "enum darq_error_code" ).
.PP
If the request can't be verified, or its method is unknown, or its
handler is NULL or fails, or builds no response, an error reply is
built in
.IR reply ,
as
.B darq.dipc.ErrorReply
is, unless the request is one-way.
A reply to send, if any, is in
.IR reply :
its length is 0 if there's none.
.I reply
may be NULL, if no reply will be sent.
.B dispatch()
returns 0, or what's wrong with the message.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " directory\fR, " \-\-directory " directory"
Write the files to
.IR directory ,
or both to standard output, header first, if it's
.BR \- .
By default, they're written next to
.IR input .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if the input can't be read, isn't valid IR, has a
format version this
.B didlcgen
doesn't support, or can't be generated; 2 if the command line is
invalid.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are reported on standard error as
.IB file ": error: " message\fR.
Generation fails if a name isn't a valid C identifier, or is a C
keyword or reserved, if two generated names would be the same, if a
message's field is named
.BR header ,
or if a struct is empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /darq/lib/c/darq/dipc.h ", " /darq/lib/c/darq/dipc.c
The DIPC runtime the generated code uses, which a program compiles with
it, with
.I /darq/lib/c
on its include path;
.I lib/c/darq
in the source tree.
.SH BUGS
C has no empty structs, so a DIDL file with one can't be generated.
.PP
There are no clients: the C runtime doesn't make p-calls yet.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR didl (5),
.BR didlc (1),
.BR didlpygen (1),
DarqOS documentation,
.IR doc/ipc.rst .
